Monado PS VR2 driver: log the headset's DisplayPort receiver status when it changes.

Why: the headset reports a `dprx_status` byte in every status packet (0 = DP link
not ready; the driver's header guesses "2 = cinematic?"). Monado keeps it only as a
debug-UI variable, so no run records what the headset thought of the stream it was
driven with. One INFO line on change (and for the first packet, whatever its value)
puts it in the service's log next to the mode and format lines a display test reads.

Applies to Monado main at ded98428 (the build PenguinScreen2 ships) and at 8b259763:
psvr2.c is identical in both. `git apply` it; `git apply -R` takes it out again.
PSVR2_INFO prints only when the driver's log level allows it: the driver's default is
WARN (`DEBUG_GET_ONCE_LOG_OPTION(psvr2_log, "PSVR2_LOG", U_LOGGING_WARN)`), so the
service needs `PSVR2_LOG=info` to show the line. Revision 2.

--- a/src/xrt/drivers/psvr2/psvr2.c
+++ b/src/xrt/drivers/psvr2/psvr2.c
@@ -304,7 +304,14 @@
 {
 	struct status_record_hdr *hdr = (struct status_record_hdr *)buf;

-	hmd->dprx_status = hdr->dprx_status;
+	static bool dprx_seen = false; // the first packet is logged whatever its value (a run that stays at 0 says so)
+	if (!dprx_seen || hmd->dprx_status != hdr->dprx_status) {
+		// 0 = DP link not ready; the values seen with a picture are not yet named (psvr2.h: "2 = cinematic?").
+		// Logged on change so a run's headset-side display state is on record next to what the kernel drove.
+		PSVR2_INFO(hmd, "DP RX status %u -> %u", hmd->dprx_status, hdr->dprx_status);
+		dprx_seen = true;
+		hmd->dprx_status = hdr->dprx_status;
+	}
 	hmd->proximity_sensor = hdr->prox_sensor_flag;
 	hmd->function_button = hdr->function_button;

